n="default = 100 amp"
N.203="variable"*72x50<M>
n="PaceCycleSetpoint Ch2 GUI"
N.204="variable"*73x67<M>
n="PaceCycleSetpoint Ch3 GUI"
N.205="variable"*72x85<M>
n="PaceCycleSetpoint Ch4 GUI"
N.206="variable"*72x102<M>
n="PaceCycleSetpoint Ch5 GUI"
N.207="const"<3>*63x50<M>
N.208="const"<3>*64x67<M>
N.209="const"<3>*64x85<M>
N.210="const"<3>*64x102<M>
N.211="variable"*72x34<M>
n="FactorCycleCountValue Ch1 GUI"
N.212="const"<120>*62x34<M>
N.213="comment"*140x21*100x71<M>
C="{\\rtf1\\ansi\\ansicpg1252\\deff0\\deflang1033{\\fonttbl{\\f0\\fnil\\fcharset0 Arial;}}
\\viewkind4\\uc1\\pard\\fs18 FactorCycleCountValue\\par
\\par
consists of 2 numbers:\\par
cycleCount =  used in the time based load controller as the cycleCount setpoint (nominally 3.5).  Good performance has been observed with this many Pace cycles per OEM cycle.\\par
factor = used in the temperature based load controller as the temperature span (nominally 1).  Increase in factor (>1) lessens the amplitude of the time change in the tVec values, A decrease does just the opposite. \\par
\\par
cycleCount must line in the range [1:.5:5]  (MUST BE A MULTIPLE OF .5)\\par
factor must lie in the range [.125:.125:1.875] (MUST BE A MULTIPLE OF .125)\\par
\\par
Algorithm:\\par
step 1: FactorCycleCountValue= (8*factor) + (32*cycleCount)  - this must be done manually and entered into the GUI\\par
\\par
step 2: decode in the PACE4\\par
convert GUI Input Value by converting it to an 8 bit number, then\\par
factor  = .125*bit0 + .25*bit1 + .5*bit2 + 1*bit3\\par
cycleCount = .5*bit4 + 1*bit5 + 2*bit6 + 3*bit7\\par
\\par
for example\\par
cycleCount = 3.5\\par
factor = .875\\par
FactorCycleCountValue = 8*.75 + 32*3.5 = 119\\par
\\par
The minimum FactorCycleCountValue = .125*8 + 1*32 = 33\\par
The maximum FactorCycleCountValue = 1.875*8 + 5*32 = 175\\par
A limit block is used to  enforce these limits in the load controllers\\par
\\par
\\par
}
"
